From a7e510857efd84c3c58d4cea97b53ae3e3999286 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ritvik Rao <rsrao2@illinois.edu>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 11:18:10 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] Shared-memory pool: one receive queue per PE and per-sender
 return lists

One queue and one heap lock per process serialized every sender and
receiver on a host: with ~3 M small messages/s per node over 2 x 23 PEs a
send took 16 us and a delivery a few hundred (Charades VLSI, Stampede3).

- Each PE of a process has its own receive queue in the process's
  segment: senders push with a compare-and-swap, the PE takes the whole
  list at once each scheduler pass and delivers it oldest first
  (CmiDeliverIpcInbox). Node datagrams have their own queue.
- Each block records the host-local index of the PE that allocated it.
  Freeing pushes it onto that sender's return list in the block's segment;
  the sender takes the list whole when its private list of that size runs
  out, and new blocks come from the heap by fetch_add.
- Every list is pushed by compare-and-swap and emptied whole by one
  exchange, so none needs a lock or an ABA counter.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
